// PRISM operational model of <iterator>. The pir stage puts this directory
// first on the C++ include path, so `#include <iterator>` reaches this file
// instead of libstdc++'s. It is a file, not a directory: a directory of this
// name would make the include fail with EISDIR and every such unit would
// fall back to libstdc++.
//
// Partial on purpose. std::begin and std::end take a reference to an array
// T (&)[N] and nothing else. begin returns a pointer to the first element
// (array-to-pointer decay, not a load). end returns a pointer one past the
// last.
//
// A zero-length array matches no overload. A parameter written T (&)[N] is
// a substitution failure for N == 0 (clang: zero-length arrays are not
// permitted in C++), and libstdc++ 14 under clang 23 rejects std::begin,
// end, data, size, ssize and empty on int[0] too, so this header does not
// accept what the library rejects.
//
// A pointer argument is not an overload of begin or end. It does not match
// T (&)[N], so the call fails to compile against this header and pir falls
// back to libstdc++. That argument is not stubbed.
//
// std::distance is only the overload for two pointers of the same type.
// The result is the element count from the first to the second. A count
// that does not fit in ptrdiff_t is __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT,
// then __builtin_trap. That path does not return a wrapped ptrdiff_t.
// This is not a proof that the two pointers address the same array.
//
// std::next is only the overloads for a pointer. next(p) returns p + 1.
// next(p, 0) returns p and does not add. next(p, n) for n > 0 returns
// p + n. n < 0 is __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT, then
// __builtin_trap. That path does not form p + n.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
//
// std::prev is only the overloads for a pointer. prev(p) returns p - 1.
// prev(p, 0) returns p and does not subtract. prev(p, n) for n > 0 returns
// p - n. n < 0 is __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT, then
// __builtin_trap. That path does not form p - n.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
//
// std::advance is only the overload for a pointer. advance(p, 0) leaves p
// unchanged and does not add or subtract. advance(p, n) for n > 0 sets p
// to p + n. advance(p, n) for n < 0 sets p to p + n, a backward step. A
// negative n does not trap and does not call next or prev.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// std::iter_swap is only the overload for two pointers of the same type.
// A null pointer is __prism_check with class PTR-NULL-DEREF and message
// "std::iter_swap null pointer", then __builtin_trap, before any load or
// store. Both null is that path, not the self path. Both non-null and
// different: the two T values are exchanged. The temporary is the value
// already stored through the first pointer. Swapping a pointer with
// itself returns and does not invent a value. This is not a proof that
// the pointers address an array. This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// std::iter_move is only the overload for one pointer. A null pointer is
// __prism_check with class PTR-NULL-DEREF and message
// "std::iter_move null pointer", then __builtin_trap, before any load.
// A non-null pointer returns the T stored through that pointer, not a
// value-initialized T{}. This is not a proof that the pointer addresses
// an array. This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// std::size is the array overload and one overload for an lvalue with a
// size member. The array overload returns the bound and does not load an
// element. N == 0 does not match that parameter, and a zero-length array
// matches no overload. A pointer argument does not match.
// There is no pointer overload. The array bodies do not call the other
// array functions. The lvalue overload returns that member once. The
// noexcept operator and the decltype operand are unevaluated. That body
// does not load an element. It does not return a fabricated 0. A class
// without that member does not match, so that call fails to compile and
// pir falls back. This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// std::empty is the array overload and one overload for an lvalue with
// an empty member. The array overload returns false and does not load an
// element. N == 0 does not match that parameter, and a zero-length array
// matches no overload. A pointer argument does not match. There is no pointer overload. The array bodies do not call size,
// begin, or end. The lvalue overload returns that member once. The
// noexcept operator and the decltype operand are unevaluated. That body
// does not call size and does not load an element. It does not return a
// fabricated false. A class without that member does not match, so that
// call fails to compile and pir falls back. This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// std::data is the array overload and one overload for an lvalue with a
// data member. The array overload returns a pointer to the first element
// by array-to-pointer decay and does not load an element. N == 0 does not
// match that parameter, and a zero-length array matches no overload. A
// pointer argument does not match.
// There is no pointer overload. The array bodies do not call the other
// array functions. The lvalue overload returns that member once. The
// noexcept operator and the decltype operand are unevaluated. That body
// does not dereference that pointer. It does not load an element. It does
// not return a fabricated nullptr. A class without that member does not
// match, so that call fails to compile and pir falls back.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// std::cdata is the array overload only. It returns a const pointer to the
// first element by array-to-pointer decay and does not load an element.
// N == 0 does not match that parameter, and a zero-length array matches no
// overload. A pointer argument does not match. There is no pointer overload. A class
// type does not match, so that call fails to compile and pir falls back.
// The bodies do not call the other array functions.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// std::ssize is the array overload and one overload for an lvalue with a
// size member. The array overload returns the bound as ptrdiff_t
// and does not load an element. A bound greater than PTRDIFF_MAX is
// __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and message
// "std::ssize array bound does not fit in ptrdiff_t", then __builtin_trap.
// That path does not cast the bound and does not return a negative value.
// N == 0 does not match that parameter, and a zero-length array matches
// no overload. A pointer argument does not match. There is no
// pointer overload. The array bodies do not call the other array
// functions. The lvalue overload reads that member once. A value greater
// than PTRDIFF_MAX is __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and message
// "std::ssize value does not fit in ptrdiff_t", then __builtin_trap. That
// path does not cast the value and does not return a negative value.
// Otherwise the result is the stored value as ptrdiff_t. That body does
// not load an element. A class without that member does not match, so
// that call fails to compile and pir falls back.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
//
// reverse_iterator is not declared.
// The rest of this header is omitted the same way (iterator traits, tags,
// insert iterators, stream iterators, and the container overloads of
// begin and end). A unit that names one fails to compile here and falls
// back. They are not stubbed.
//
// This is not a proof that an iterator stays in range. There is no check
// on a later increment or on a dereference of the returned pointer.
//
// This file includes <cstddef> and <cstdint>. It does not include <memory>,
// <ranges>, <algorithm>, or <cmath>.
#ifndef _GLIBCXX_ITERATOR
#define _GLIBCXX_ITERATOR 1

#pragma GCC system_header

#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>

// PIR intrinsic (translate.cpp). Literal class and message. Declared, not
// defined: the analysed program does not link a body.
extern "C" void __prism_check(int, const char*, const char*);

namespace std {

// The failing path never returns. A wrapping subtraction of the two
// addresses is not produced here.
[[noreturn]] inline void _Prism_distance_does_not_fit() noexcept {
    __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::distance subtraction does not fit in ptrdiff_t");
    __builtin_trap();
}

// Element count from __first to __last. The address gap is an unsigned
// magnitude, then divided by sizeof(_Tp). A magnitude outside
// [PTRDIFF_MIN, PTRDIFF_MAX] takes the check above. This is not a proof
// that the two pointers address the same array.
template <typename _Tp>
std::ptrdiff_t _Prism_pointer_diff(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last) noexcept {
    static_assert(sizeof(_Tp) != 0, "distance requires a complete object type");
    const auto __uf = reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(__first);
    const auto __ul = reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(__last);
    const bool __neg = __ul < __uf;
    const std::uintptr_t __bytes = __neg ? (__uf - __ul) : (__ul - __uf);
    const std::uintptr_t __n = __bytes / static_cast<std::uintptr_t>(sizeof(_Tp));
    const std::uintptr_t __max = static_cast<std::uintptr_t>(PTRDIFF_MAX);
    if (__neg) {
        // PTRDIFF_MIN is representable. Its magnitude is one past PTRDIFF_MAX.
        // A larger magnitude would be a wrapped bit pattern. Do not return it.
        if (__n > __max) {
            if (__n != __max + 1) _Prism_distance_does_not_fit();
            return PTRDIFF_MIN;
        }
        return static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(0) - static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(__n);
    }
    if (__n > __max) _Prism_distance_does_not_fit();
    return static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(__n);
}

// Pointer to the first element. The bound is at least 1: N == 0 does not
// match this parameter.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr _Tp* begin(_Tp (&__arr)[_Nm]) noexcept {
    return __arr;
}

// One past the last element. The addition is the bound. N == 0 does not
// match this parameter.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr _Tp* end(_Tp (&__arr)[_Nm]) noexcept {
    return __arr + _Nm;
}

// Element count. The bound is at least 1: N == 0 does not match this
// parameter. The result is the bound. The body does not load an element.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr size_t size(_Tp (&__arr)[_Nm]) noexcept {
    return _Nm;
}

// An lvalue with a size member. That member is returned once. The
// noexcept operator and the decltype operand are unevaluated. The body
// does not load an element. It does not return a fabricated 0. An array
// does not match. A pointer does not match.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr auto size(const _Tp& __t) noexcept(noexcept(__t.size())) -> decltype(__t.size()) {
    return __t.size();
}

// False for a bound of at least 1. N == 0 does not match this
// parameter. The parameter is unnamed, so the body does not load an
// element. The result is false.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr bool empty(_Tp (&)[_Nm]) noexcept {
    return false;
}

// An lvalue with an empty member. That member is returned once. The
// noexcept operator and the decltype operand are unevaluated. The body
// does not call size. It does not load an element. It does not return a
// fabricated false. An array does not match. A pointer does not match.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr auto empty(const _Tp& __t) noexcept(noexcept(__t.empty())) -> decltype(__t.empty()) {
    return __t.empty();
}

// Pointer to the first element. The bound is at least 1: N == 0 does not
// match this parameter. The result is array-to-pointer decay, not a load.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr _Tp* data(_Tp (&__arr)[_Nm]) noexcept {
    return __arr;
}

// An lvalue with a data member. That member is returned once. The
// noexcept operator and the decltype operand are unevaluated. The body
// does not dereference that pointer. It does not load an element. It
// does not return a fabricated nullptr. An array does not match. A
// pointer does not match.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr auto data(const _Tp& __t) noexcept(noexcept(__t.data())) -> decltype(__t.data()) {
    return __t.data();
}

// Const pointer to the first element. The bound is at least 1: N == 0 does
// not match this parameter. The result is array-to-pointer decay, not a load.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr const _Tp* cdata(_Tp (&__arr)[_Nm]) noexcept {
    return __arr;
}

// A bound that does not fit in ptrdiff_t. This path does not cast that
// bound and does not return a negative value.
[[noreturn]] inline void _Prism_ssize_array_bound_does_not_fit() noexcept {
    __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::ssize array bound does not fit in ptrdiff_t");
    __builtin_trap();
}

// The bound is at least 1: N == 0 does not match this parameter. A bound
// greater than PTRDIFF_MAX takes the check above and is not cast.
// Otherwise the result is that bound as ptrdiff_t. The parameter is
// unnamed, so the body does not load an element.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr std::ptrdiff_t ssize(_Tp (&)[_Nm]) noexcept {
    if constexpr (_Nm > static_cast<size_t>(PTRDIFF_MAX)) {
        _Prism_ssize_array_bound_does_not_fit();
    } else {
        return static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(_Nm);
    }
}

// A stored value that does not fit in ptrdiff_t. This path does not cast
// that value and does not return a negative value.
[[noreturn]] inline void _Prism_ssize_value_does_not_fit() noexcept {
    __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::ssize value does not fit in ptrdiff_t");
    __builtin_trap();
}

// An lvalue with a size member. That member is read once into a local.
// A value greater than PTRDIFF_MAX takes the check above and is not cast.
// Otherwise the result is that stored value as ptrdiff_t. The body does
// not load an element. An array does not match. A pointer does not match.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
    requires true  // distinct from (and preferred over) the libstdc++ bits/range_access.h overload
constexpr auto ssize(const _Tp& __t) noexcept(noexcept(__t.size())) -> decltype(__t.size(), std::ptrdiff_t()) {
    const auto __n = __t.size();
    if (__n > PTRDIFF_MAX) {
        _Prism_ssize_value_does_not_fit();
    } else {
        return static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(__n);
    }
}

// Two pointers only. A class iterator does not match, so the call fails
// to compile and pir falls back. Constant evaluation uses the subtraction:
// a count that does not fit in ptrdiff_t is not a constant expression, so
// that return is not a wrapped value. At run time the count is the unsigned
// distance, and a value that does not fit is the check, not a wrapped result.
// This is not a proof that the two pointers address the same array.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr std::ptrdiff_t distance(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last) noexcept {
    if consteval {
        return __last - __first;
    }
    return _Prism_pointer_diff(__first, __last);
}

// n < 0. This path does not form p + n.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
[[noreturn]] inline void _Prism_next_negative() noexcept {
    __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::next offset is negative");
    __builtin_trap();
}

// Pointer only. There is no default argument: next(p) is this overload,
// and it returns p + 1. A class iterator does not match, so that call
// fails to compile and pir falls back.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* next(_Tp* __p) noexcept {
    return __p + 1;
}

// next(p, 0) returns p and does not add. n > 0 returns p + n. n < 0
// calls the check above and does not form p + n.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* next(_Tp* __p, std::ptrdiff_t __n) noexcept {
    if (__n < 0) {
        _Prism_next_negative();
    } else if (__n == 0) {
        return __p;
    } else {
        return __p + __n;
    }
}

// n < 0. This path does not form p - n.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
[[noreturn]] inline void _Prism_prev_negative() noexcept {
    __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::prev offset is negative");
    __builtin_trap();
}

// Pointer only. There is no default argument: prev(p) is this overload,
// and it returns p - 1. A class iterator does not match, so that call
// fails to compile and pir falls back.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* prev(_Tp* __p) noexcept {
    return __p - 1;
}

// prev(p, 0) returns p and does not subtract. n > 0 returns p - n. n < 0
// calls the check above and does not form p - n.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* prev(_Tp* __p, std::ptrdiff_t __n) noexcept {
    if (__n < 0) {
        _Prism_prev_negative();
    } else if (__n == 0) {
        return __p;
    } else {
        return __p - __n;
    }
}

// Pointer only. There is no default argument. advance(__p, 0) leaves __p
// unchanged and does not add or subtract. n > 0 sets __p to __p + __n.
// n < 0 sets __p to __p + __n, a backward step. That path does not trap
// and does not call next or prev. A class iterator does not match, so
// that call fails to compile and pir falls back.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr void advance(_Tp*& __p, std::ptrdiff_t __n) noexcept {
    if (__n == 0) {
        return;
    } else if (__n > 0) {
        __p = __p + __n;
    } else if (__n < 0) {
        __p = __p + __n;
    }
}

// A null pointer. This path does not load or store.
// Both null takes this path, not the self path.
// This is not a proof that the pointers address an array.
[[noreturn]] inline void _Prism_iter_swap_null() noexcept {
    __prism_check(0, "PTR-NULL-DEREF", "std::iter_swap null pointer");
    __builtin_trap();
}

// Two pointers only. A null pointer calls the check above before any load
// or store. Both non-null and different: exchange the two T values. The
// temporary is the T already stored through __a, not a value-initialized
// T. The same pointer twice returns without a load or a store, so the
// existing value stays. That path does not invent a value. A class
// iterator does not match, so that call fails to compile and pir falls
// back.
// This is not a proof that the pointers address an array.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr void iter_swap(_Tp* __a, _Tp* __b) noexcept {
    if (__a == nullptr || __b == nullptr) {
        _Prism_iter_swap_null();
    } else if (__a == __b) {
        return;
    } else {
        _Tp __tmp = *__a;
        *__a = *__b;
        *__b = __tmp;
    }
}

// A null pointer. This path does not load.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
[[noreturn]] inline void _Prism_iter_move_null() noexcept {
    __prism_check(0, "PTR-NULL-DEREF", "std::iter_move null pointer");
    __builtin_trap();
}

// One pointer only. A null pointer calls the check above before any load.
// A non-null pointer returns the T stored through __p. That return is the
// load, not a value-initialized T. A class iterator does not match, so
// that call fails to compile and pir falls back.
// This is not a proof that the pointer addresses an array.
// This is not PROVED and not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp iter_move(_Tp* __p) noexcept {
    if (__p == nullptr) {
        _Prism_iter_move_null();
    } else {
        return *__p;
    }
}

}  // namespace std

#endif  // _GLIBCXX_ITERATOR
